hat it only has ever been  radicals that have changed this country  yeah if that's what radical means call  me a radical so of course this  is a top marginal tax rate meaning that  you would see a progressively higher tax  as income goes up the right like to say  when it comes to sort of understanding  the underlying philosophies of taxation  the right likes to say that taxes should  be low on the rich because they create  jobs because the rich having more money  trickles down to the lower economy and  it raises all boats like a rising tide  this is called effectively supply-side  economics the left and people who  understand reality quite frankly  understand that there is a diminishing  marginal utility to dollars what that  means is if you have ten dollars to your  name an additional ten dollars doubles  your net worth that's huge if you have a  hundred dollars to your name an  additional ten dollars is useful but  it's not as useful to you as to the  person who only has ten dollars to begin  with if you have a million dollars ten  dollars is nothing right it's interest  on a savings account it's a rounding  error it's nothing and in the same way  the more money you have the more likely  you are to just let any extra money that  you obtain sit in a savings account  which is not stimulative to the economy  as much as giving the money to poorer  people would be who would be more likely  to spend the money and inject it into  the economy this is called demand side  economics at a certain point the  marginal utility of another dollar is  zero if you're Bill Gates or Warren  Buffett or Mark Zuckerberg and you have  billions of dollars a one percentage  point increase in your taxes which  leaves you with one percentage point  less money at the end of the year it has  zero impact on your life maybe it's not  literally zero but it's Oh point zero  zero zero zero zero one percent of each  dollar actually impacts your life and  your financial decisions in any way that  is what a higher top marginal tax rate  capitalizes on that for the very rich  those additional dollars do nothing for  them but they would do a lot for other  people for the  for social programs etc so now that we  understand the idea let's look at the  data about the history of these very  high or higher top marginal tax rates  the u.s. used to have more tax brackets  and they used to have higher tax  brackets at the top so under Dwight D  Eisenhower as president the top tax  bracket was 91% this went down to 50%  under Reagan this went down to 38% after  the 1986 tax reform with more tax  brackets you can have different top  marginal tax rates for someone earning  five hundred thousand dollars a year a  million dollars a year ten million a  hundred million right now everybody  earning over five hundred and ten or so  thousand dollars a year pays thirty  seven percent okay that's it the highest  bracket starts at five hundred and ten  thousand dollars in income and there's  nothing else above that it's thirty  seven percent all the way up so then we  get to what this policy and economic  research tell us the top marginal tax  rate should be well the question is what  are you trying to achieve the right is  correct when they say if you keep  raising that top tax rate at a certain  point it will stop people from working  at all  which the government doesn't want  because then they make zero right so if  you go to high it actually reduces  government revenue what MIT research has  found is that a top marginal tax rate of  seventy three percent is the point at  which the government will maximize  revenue by taking advantage of that very  high-end for whom those additional  dollars have very little utility there's  complex math around it which we don't  need to get into but the point is a top  marginal tax rate of 70 percent as sort  of floated by alexandria castillo cortez  if you set it up with the correct  brackets would be something that raises  a ton of money for programs that would  actually improve our society so that is  the the sort of history of the tax  brackets now lastly some republicans  seemed  not understand that this is just a  marginal tax rate it's not the rate  anybody pays on all their income so as  an example Republican Congressman Steve  Scalise tweeted over the weekend about  Alexandria Acacio Cortez's interview  with 60 minutes where she talked about  this he said Republicans let Americans  keep more of their own hard-earned money  Democrats take away 70% of your income  and give it to leftist fantasy programs  no one wants to take seventy percent of  your money based on a 70 percent top  marginal tax rate okay everybody pays  the same rate on the first five hundred  and ten thousand dollars that they earn  you then put in place higher rates for  money earned beyond that now I don't  know if Steve Scalise and other  Republicans who are saying this stuff I  don't know if they don't understand how  marginal tax rates work or if they're  deliberately playing dumb to incite  their followers but let's explain it  really really simply okay I put together  the 2019 actual tax brackets  with some hypothetical higher tax  brackets that would get us up to 70  percent marginal tax okay so in reality  right now everybody pays 10 percent on  income up to ninety seven hundred  dollars everybody doesn't matter if you  made a million or ten million on your  first ninety seven hundred dollars you  pay 10% likewise you have rates of  twelve twenty to twenty four thirty to  thirty five and thirty seven percent on  other income ranges so if you make a  hundred thousand dollars a year you pay  twenty two percent on everything between  forty and eighty five thousand and then  for what you earn between it's exactly  80 for 201 and a hundred thousand you  pay twenty four percent on your first  eighty four thousand dollars in income  you pay the same as everybody who makes  less than you and everybody who makes  more than you so the 